                        <description><![CDATA[<div class=paige-shortcode-figure><div class="align-items-center d-flex float-start h-100 justify-content-center me-4"><figure class=mb-0><div class="d-flex justify-content-center text-center"></div></figure></div></div><p>Hi! I&rsquo;m Michael Milazzo, a 5th year Ph.D. candidate in the <a href=https://sites.google.com/berkeley.edu/goldstein-lab rel=external>Goldstein Group</a>
at UC Berkeley.
Prior to this, I worked at the California Air Resources Board and earned a M.S. from Virginia Tech under <a href=https://air.cee.vt.edu rel=external>Linsey Marr</a>
.</p><p>My research focuses on characterizing anthropogenic emissions of organic compounds into the atmosphere and understanding their effects on secondary pollutant formation.</p><p>I am particularly interested in policy-relevant emissions that have traditionally been ignored, such as those from fires at the Wildland-Urban Interface.</p>]]></description>
                    

                    
